{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observed\nOn 2026-09-02 the configured rebar MCP endpoint initially accepted tracker calls and started plan-review job 1788399093580353346-ab9badfce4eb4b45a6db70794296455e. A later status poll returned \"Transport closed\"; every subsequent rebar MCP call returned nginx HTTP 502. The local CLI remained functional.\n\n**Corrected from the original report:** the outage was not \"more than 20 minutes\". It ran ~18:31 PDT 09-02 to ~06:41 PDT 09-03 — about **12 hours**, with no self-recovery. A human reported it; nothing in the system could observe it.\n\n## Impact\nAgents cannot poll gate results, claim work, or perform tracker operations through the required MCP surface. Long gate state is ambiguous and restarting it risks duplicate billed work.\n\n## Root cause (confirmed on-box, read-only)\n1. The MCP container was **OOM-killed ~3 minutes into a plan-review gate run** — `docker inspect` reported `OOMKilled=true`, `Exit=137`, finished 18:36:51 PDT against a job started 18:31:33 PDT. Host is a t4g.large (8 GiB) shared with Gerrit, the review-bot and opcert.\n2. nginx's `upstream rebar_mcp` is a **single `server` line with no failover**, and still named the dead container's port, so every request 502'd while nginx itself stayed healthy.\n3. Recovery was structurally impossible: redeploy is the only path back, and every build failed on `uv required-version ==0.12.7 does not match 0.12.9` (split out as `febd-6b13-1976-43be`, now closed).\n4. `mcp_retire_sweep` reaped the exited-but-live container while nginx still pointed at its port, converting a transient crash into a permanent 502.\n\nRuled OUT: `mcp_mem_abort` and `mcp_retire_cap` both read 0.0 throughout. They are deploy-path markers, not observers of the serving process, so a kernel OOM-kill is invisible to them.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Start an async plan review through MCP.\n2. Poll `gate_status` for its job handle.\n3. Observe transport closure followed by persistent HTTP 502 responses.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] AC1: an unhealthy MCP upstream is detected and reported with an actionable signal. Verified by `aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --alarm-names rebar-mcp-serving-path-down` showing the alarm live on metric `mcp_healthy`, and by `aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace rebar/host --metric-name mcp_healthy` returning datapoints.\n- [x] AC2: a regression test covers loss of the MCP upstream. Verified by `python -m pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_mcp_health_9ea3.py -q`, which drives the real `observability.sh` against stubbed 502/timeout responses and asserts a `0` is published rather than nothing.\n- [x] AC3: a transient MCP crash can no longer become a permanent 502 via the retire sweep. Verified by `python -m pytest tests/scripts -k retire -q`, including a case that reproduces the incident signature (the sweep freeing the port nginx still names).\n- [ ] AC4: host and per-container memory, and peak RSS per gate run, are measured and queryable, so a memory limit or instance size can be chosen from data rather than guessed. Verified by `python -m pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_memory_9ea3.py tests/unit/test_gate_peak_rss_9ea3.py -q`, and post-deploy by `aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace rebar/host --metric-name mem_available_percent` returning datapoints and a `GATE_PEAK_RSS` marker appearing in `journalctl` after a gate run.\n- [ ] AC5 [non-codebase]: the measurement campaign has produced the numbers the OOM decision needs — peak MCP RSS during a gate, steady-state RSS per container, and how RSS scales with ticket size. Verified by a comment on this ticket recording those figures.\n\n**Deliberately NOT in this ticket, per operator decision:** choosing a container `mem_limit`, an instance size, or a fan-out width. Every candidate value today is a guess; the decision waits on AC5's data. ADRs 0079 and 0104 explicitly decided against upsizing to t4g.xlarge, and `3276`'s operator-approved principle — growing a resource is an unwedge, not a fix — applies identically. Moving gate execution off-box is the honest end-state and is epic-sized.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_mcp_health_9ea3.py -q` — the serving-path probe.\n- `python -m pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_memory_9ea3.py tests/unit/test_gate_peak_rss_9ea3.py -q` — the memory instrumentation.\n- `python -m pytest tests/scripts -q` — the full shell-probe suite.\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py -q` — the alarm guards, including the AWS description-length limit.\n- `make lint && make typecheck`.\n- Post-deploy: `aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --alarm-names rebar-mcp-serving-path-down` and `get-metric-statistics` on `mcp_healthy`, `mem_available_percent` and `container_memory_rss_bytes`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788465407797124001,"uuid":"fdc21589-ccd8-4706-a057-043725e5775b"}